## Quick Reference: Security Checklist

- [ ] Use parameterized queries for all database operations
- [ ] Sanitize and validate all user inputs
- [ ] Hash passwords with bcrypt (min 10 rounds)
- [ ] Implement JWT token expiration and refresh mechanisms
- [ ] Use HTTPS only (enforce with HSTS headers)
- [ ] Implement proper CORS with whitelisted origins
- [ ] Add rate limiting to prevent brute force attacks
- [ ] Use helmet.js for security headers
- [ ] Store secrets in environment variables only
- [ ] Implement comprehensive error handling without info disclosure
- [ ] Keep dependencies updated and scan for vulnerabilities
- [ ] Implement role-based access control (RBAC)
- [ ] Add multi-factor authentication for sensitive operations
- [ ] Use HTTPOnly and Secure cookies for sessions
- [ ] Implement comprehensive security logging
- [ ] Set up real-time alerting for suspicious activities
- [ ] Use Content Security Policy (CSP) headers
- [ ] Implement CSRF protection with tokens
- [ ] Validate file uploads (type, size, contents)
- [ ] Use security headers (X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, etc.)

---

## Recommended Security Packages

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "express": "^4.18.0",
    "helmet": "^6.0.0",          // Security headers
    "bcrypt": "^5.1.0",          // Password hashing
    "jsonwebtoken": "^9.0.0",    // JWT tokens
    "express-validator": "^7.0.0", // Input validation
    "xss": "^1.0.0",             // XSS protection
    "express-rate-limit": "^6.0.0", // Rate limiting
    "cors": "^2.8.5",            // CORS configuration
    "dotenv": "^16.0.0",         // Environment variables
    "winston": "^3.0.0",         // Logging
    "joi": "^17.0.0",            // Schema validation
    "express-mongo-sanitize": "^2.0.0" // NoSQL injection prevention
  }
}
```

---

## Final Notes

Security is not a one-time implementation but an ongoing process. Regular security audits, dependency updates, and monitoring are essential. Consider:

1. Regular security training for your team
2. Conducting penetration testing
3. Using SAST/DAST tools in your CI/CD pipeline
4. Implementing security headers and CSP policies
5. Following OWASP Top 10 guidelines
6. Staying updated with security advisories
7. Implementing incident response procedures

